      "id": "we-need-to-talk-how-to-have-conversations-that-matter-ch-6",
      "order": 6,
      "chapterNumber": 6,
      "headline": "Be There or Go Elsewhere",
      "keyInsight": "Multitasking in human conversation is a mathematical myth. You cannot clean out your inbox with one eye while offering your soul with the other. Human beings possess an ancient, built-in radar for detecting fake attention. The second your eyes flicker toward a screen or your mind wanders to a grocery list, the other person registers emotional abandonment. The great paradox of modern communication is that half-presence takes more energy than total immersion. Hovering between two worlds leaves you drained and leaves your companion alienated. Either step completely inside the circle of conversation or excuse yourself entirely. Wholehearted presence is the rarest gift you own.",
      "content": "Imagine asking a mechanic to inspect your car engine while they text a friend with one hand. You would immediately doubt their skill and take your car elsewhere. Yet we do this exact thing every day to people we love. We nod along while mentally planning dinner, checking wristwatches, or skimming email alerts.\n\nYour brain can only focus on one conscious stream of meaning at a time. When you try to toggle between a speaker and a gadget, you drop conversational frames. The other person notices the micro-delays in your eyes. Even if you repeat their last five words, they feel the hollow echo.\n\nPresence is binary: you are either fully anchored or drifting at sea. If you cannot give your whole attention right now, be honest and reschedule. A twenty-minute conversation with complete devotion beats two hours of absentminded nodding every single time.",
