      "id": "we-need-to-talk-how-to-have-conversations-that-matter-ch-7",
      "order": 7,
      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "Stop Stealing the Spotlight With Your Own Stories",
      "keyInsight": "When a friend shares a struggle, your instinct is to jump in with your own tale. You think you are building a bridge of empathy. In reality, you are hijacking their moment. Sociologists call this shift response versus support response. A shift response swerves the steering wheel right back to you. If someone tells you about their difficult boss, and you say, 'I had a terrible boss once too,' you just stole the microphone. Empathy is not proving that you survived the exact same storm. Real empathy is pulling up a chair and sitting in their rain. It feels counterintuitive because our brains love sharing personal memories. But your experience is never identical to theirs. Assuming it is trivializes their unique pain. True connection requires stepping back and letting the other person own the floor.",
      "content": "Imagine you are helping a friend tune a guitar. They pluck a string to check the pitch, but instead of listening, you rip into a loud solo on your own guitar. That is what happens when someone shares a problem and you reply with your own story. You might think you are demonstrating solidarity. In truth, you just turned their personal moment into your open-mic night.\n\nThink of a conversation like passing a fragile glass bowl back and forth. When someone confides in you, they hand you their bowl. Your job is to hold it gently and look at it. If you immediately hand them your bowl instead, you force them to juggle both. Good listeners practice support responses rather than shift responses. A shift response says, 'I went through that too.' A support response asks, 'How did that feel for you?'\n\nNo two human journeys ever match up perfectly. When you tell a friend that your broken leg was just like their sprained ankle, you shrink their experience. Treat every conversation like visiting a foreign garden. You are not there to brag about your own backyard vegetables. You are there to admire their unique plants, ask curious questions, and let them be the expert on their own life.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Never match someone else's hardship with your own story; keep the focus entirely on them.",
      "audioSnippetText": "When someone shares a pain, resist the urge to say you went through the same thing. Keep the attention on their story instead of hijacking the microphone.",
